{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"Cross-subsidization can run either way: ETF trading or basket activity may impose costs borne by the shared portfolio, diluting mutual-fund shareholders; conversely, mutual-fund subscriptions/redemptions or other portfolio activity may impose costs borne by the portfolio and indirectly by ETF shareholders. These are conceptual examples, not findings that either class necessarily subsidizes the other.\n\nFor monitoring, distinguish **transaction costs** (such as commissions, spreads, market impact, and costs of implementing portfolio trades), **cash** (cash levels and flows that may require investment or redemption), and **gains** (realized gains and potential tax effects). A representative order-specific framework evaluates an individual order’s expected costs and effects across classes and the portfolio, considering relevant circumstances and allocation—not a universal numeric cutoff. The categories guide review; they do not establish that every order is problematic or prescribe one-size-fits-all thresholds."}}
